## Decided ideas (most recent first)
- D-015 on R-018: **refuted** — End-to-end: a single-phase, rollout-searched exploration lane raises the search's best held-out success
  R-018 added an exploration-lane option (config/two_lane_search_v2.yaml): single-phase generation with code-derived idea records, treated by 24-candidate independent rollout search on the probe. Six paired runs per configuration (seeds 3100-3111), incumbents scored on per-block fresh manifests (pool seeds 3130-3135). v2 minus control incumbent success: -0.007, run-level [-0.035, +0.022]; the predicted +0.05 is refuted. Every incumbent in all twelve runs came from the unchanged fittable lane at 1.00 promotion success, so the task is saturated and exploration candidates could only tie. The new lane itself works: exploration screen success rose from 0.03 to 0.78 on average (best per run 0.10 to 0.97) with fewer LLM calls (10 vs 11-12).
  Next step then proposed: Keep v2 as an option, not the default, on push-v2; evaluate the v2 exploration lane where no seed bank saturates the task (a harder MetaWorld task, or the fittable lane disabled), and confirm its screen winners on fresh cases.
- D-014 on R-017: **supported** — Single-phase seedless generation beats the two-phase ideas prompt under rollout search
  R-017 compared seedless generation by the single-phase prompt with the two-phase ideas-then-implementation generator the exploration lane and the gpt-6.1-sol baseline use, four blocks, one structure per generator per block (13 gpt-6.1-sol calls), each given 24-candidate independent rollout search under two seeds (probe s3046, confirmation s3047). Single-phase minus two-phase: +0.698, block-level [+0.395, +1.000]; single-phase won every block (0.44-1.00 vs 0.00-0.32). Untrained, two-phase scored 0.00 everywhere (as in the baseline run) and single-phase 0.00-0.90. Supported: the generator bundle (prompt wording plus design/implementation split), not phasing alone, is what the result isolates; part of the gain exists before search (+0.25).
  Next step then proposed: Switch the exploration lane to single-phase generation plus 24-candidate rollout search and rerun the end-to-end search against the current configuration on a fresh confirmation set.
- D-013 on R-015: **refuted** — Generate fit-ready structures without supplying a parent
  R-015 appended a parameterization contract (smooth sigmoid gates, unit-direction drives scaled by bounded gains, 6-10 bounded parameters) to the single-phase seedless generation prompt and compared it with the same prompt without the contract: three blocks, one gpt-6.1-sol structure per prompt (all valid first time), each given 24-candidate independent rollout search under two seeds (probe s3025, confirmation s3026). Contract minus baseline: -0.293, block-level [-0.655, +0.069], placement-level [-0.365, -0.221]; worse in every block, untrained and searched. The predicted +0.10 is refuted. Side observation: the plain single-phase prompt's structures scored 0.16-0.88 untrained and 0.80-1.00 after search, against 0.00 for the two-phase gpt-6.1-sol baseline run.
  Next step then proposed: Test single-phase versus two-phase seedless generation under rollout search with more blocks; if it holds, switch the exploration lane's prompt and add rollout search.
- D-012 on R-016: **inconclusive** — Adapt parameter proposals using progress before success appears
  R-016 compared progress-guided adaptive parameter search (two batches of 8 from a Gaussian over the top-3 candidates) with budget-matched independent local/global draws, 24 candidates per arm with the first 8 shared, on two weak evolved structures x two search seeds (5-case probe s3032, confirmation s3033). Adaptive minus independent: +0.070 placement-level [+0.038, +0.102], 14/0/36, but cell-level [-0.069, +0.209]; the predicted +0.08 is neither supported nor refuted. All gains are on baseline61_gen0_cand2_a1 (0.94 vs 0.76, 0.10 vs 0.00); gen0_cand1_a0 tied on a shared-batch pick. Notably, 24-candidate rollout search lifted these 0.00-zero-shot structures to 0.42-0.94 in three of four cells.
  Next step then proposed: Confirm on more weak structures with a larger probe before changing the exploration lane; 24-candidate rollout search itself looks like a strong rescue for zero-shot structures.
- D-011 on R-014: **refuted** — Ablate the geometry child's added trainable dimensions
  R-014 clamped the geometry child's only added parameter (approach_standoff, fixed at its declared 0.02) and compared it with the fully trainable child under identical fitting (seeds 3010/3011, probe s3012, confirmation s3013). Both reach 1.00 under both seeds: +0.000 [0, 0], 0/0/50. The predicted +0.05 is refuted: the child's advantage over simple_damped_push (D-006) comes from its changed forward geometry with a fixed 0.02 standoff, not from fitting the added dimension.
  Next step then proposed: A hand-written simple_damped_push variant with a fixed 0.02 standoff would reproduce the gain without an LLM; R-016 (adaptive search) is next.
- D-010 on R-009: **refuted** — Test LLM bounds-only edits against matched random bounds
  R-009 let gpt-6.1-sol narrow at most two parameter ranges (no code change) of two seed-bank parents, given the unedited fit's probe trajectory, and compared against width-matched random ranges and the unedited fit, all fitted identically (fit seeds 2823/2824, probe s2820, confirmation s2822). The LLM made no edit for simple_damped_push and raised the contact_radius floor for shared_gain; LLM minus random +0.000 [+0.000, +0.000], 0/0/50, and both edited arms equal the unedited fit within noise (+0.010 [-0.004, +0.024]). The predicted +0.05 is refuted; the bounds rarely bind under rollout-selected fitting.
  Next step then proposed: LLM interventions on the fitted seed bank (bounded mutation, repair, scope edits, bounds) are null or negative except one structure; prefer a ruling on admitting gen0_cand5_a0 and changes to the search pipeline itself.
- D-009 on R-013: **inconclusive** — Bank-admission check: the geometry child beats its parent on the promotion set (development 10-39)
  R-013 rebuilt the predeclared seed-2900 fitted states of the geometry child gen0_cand5_a0 and its parent simple_damped_push by deterministic refit (no rollouts; both parameter hashes match R-010's records) and scored both on the promotion set (development 10-39). Child 1.000, parent 0.967: +0.033 [-0.035, +0.102], 1/0/29. The interval spans zero, so the bank-admission requirement (strictly positive lower bound) is not met, and the refutation condition is not met either. The parent fails only one promotion case, a ceiling that 30 cases cannot resolve; the child's advantage stands on the fresh-case evidence of D-004 and D-006.
  Next step then proposed: Bank admission of gen0_cand5_a0 needs a ruling on whether fresh-case evidence may substitute for the promotion-set rule; R-009 (LLM bounds-only edits) is approved and next.
- D-008 on R-012: **refuted** — Transfer geometry-only mutation beyond damped push
  R-012 asked the LLM (gpt-6.1-sol, 9 calls) for code-only mutations of two reach-push seed-bank parents, two geometry-only and two gain/damping-only per parent, all valid, and fitted each child and parent identically under two seeds (probe s2922, confirmation s2923). Geometry minus gain/damping: -0.237 [-0.315, -0.160]; geometry minus parent: -0.193 [-0.234, -0.151]; gain/damping minus parent: +0.045 [-0.019, +0.109]. Three of four geometry children damaged a working controller (e.g. sharp_gate 0.98 -> 0.54). The damped-push geometry win (D-004, D-006) does not transfer as a scope rule; caveat: these edits were code-only, while the winning child added parameters.
  Next step then proposed: Treat gen0_cand5_a0 as a single validated structure; run its cheap bank-admission check; do not spend further on scope rules.

